@section 19.2 | Foreign Investment

@q 13:8-10

@qnote 13:8 | When asked (2006), **telecommunications** (d) was taken as the answer. The source keys the present answer, **(b) services**, the top FDI sector from April 2000 to March 2025 (about 16%), followed by computer software and hardware.

@q 13:11-13

@qnote 13:13 | **Mauritius** (d) was the largest source when asked (2010). The source keys the present answer, **(b) Singapore**, the top source for the last seven years.

@q 13:14-17

@table
FDI fact | Detail
Largest source (2024-25) | **Singapore** (about US$ 15 billion), then **Mauritius**, USA, Netherlands, UAE
Why Singapore | The **2016 India-Mauritius tax treaty** amendment ended Mauritius's capital-gains benefit
Top sectors (2000-2025) | **Services**, computer software and hardware, trading, telecom, automobiles
Not allowed | **Lottery, gambling and betting, chit funds, Nidhi companies**, real estate business, tobacco products, atomic energy
**Insurance** | 26% → 49% (2015) → 74% (2021) → **100%** (announced Budget 2025-26)
Multi-brand retail | 51% (2012, with conditions); single-brand **100%**
**Participatory notes** | Issued by registered **FPIs** to foreign investors who invest without registering with SEBI
FPI limit in corporate bonds | Raised from 9% to **15%** (Budget 2020-21)
Small-scale units (1990s) | Foreign equity up to **24%**
@end

@section 19.3 | External Debt

@q 13:18-22

@table
External debt fact | Detail
Size | About **US$ 746 billion** (end-September 2025); was over **US$ 300 billion** in 2010
Largest component | **Commercial borrowings** (about 35%), then currency and deposits (NRI deposits)
**ECBs** | Regulated by the **RBI** (under FEMA)
World Bank class | **Less indebted** country
Debt-GDP | About **19-20%** of GDP
Most indebted EMDEs (2024) | **China, Brazil, India**, Mexico, Turkiye
@end

@q 13:23-24

**Green bonds** fund climate-friendly projects; the first was issued by the **European Investment Bank** (2007), and they are long-term, not short-dated. India issued **sovereign green bonds** in 2023. The **Oil Pool Account** was closed on **1 April 2002**, when petroleum prices began to be freed.

