@q 13:29-30

@src1 13:9
@one U.P.U.D.A./L.D.A. (Pre) 2001
Euro is the national currency of
@ans 20 States of European Union
@end

The **euro** was launched on **1 January 1999** as a book (electronic) currency; notes and coins came on **1 January 2002**. **Bulgaria** adopted it on **1 January 2026** as the **21st** member of the eurozone. The ECB is in **Frankfurt**.

@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Mains) 2007
The Government of India has replaced FERA by :
(a) The Competition Act
(b) The FEMA
(c) The Monopolies Act
(d) The MRTP Act
@ans (b)
@note **FERA** (1973) treated forex offences as criminal; **FEMA** (passed **1999**, in force **1 June 2000**) made them civil and aims to **manage**, not control, foreign exchange. FEMA was "finally implemented" when FERA's sunset period ended in **2002**.
@end
@src 13:31

@q 13:32-33

@up UP link: Much of UP's FDI goes to **Noida and Greater Noida**: electronics (Samsung's phone factory, the world's largest), data centres and semiconductors (HCL-Foxconn unit at Jewar). Remittances flow largely to **eastern UP** (Gorakhpur, Azamgarh, Jaunpur) from the Gulf.

@trap
**Flexible rate = market, not central bank.** **Current account convertibility 1994; Tarapore = capital account.** **Devaluation: 1949, 1966, 1991 (twice).** **India = top remittance receiver.** **Singapore = top FDI source now (Mauritius earlier).** **Services = top FDI sector.** **P-notes = FPIs.** **No FDI in lottery, gambling, chit funds.** **ECB = RBI.** **Largest debt part = commercial borrowing.** **Forex reserves: FCA > gold > SDR > IMF tranche.** **FERA → FEMA (1999/2000).** **Euro 1999; notes 2002.**
@end

